{"passage": "The Great Barrier Reef, located off the coast of Queensland, Australia, is the world's largest coral reef system. Composed of over 2,900 individual reefs and 900 islands, it stretches for over 2,300 kilometers. This natural wonder supports a diverse array of marine life, including fish, molluscs, and sea turtles. Climate change and pollution have threatened its existence, leading to coral bleaching events in recent years.", "question": "What is the location of the Great Barrier Reef?", "answers": [{"text": "Off the coast of Queensland, Australia", "label": 1}, {"text": "In the Atlantic Ocean", "label": 0}, {"text": "Near New Zealand", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants convert sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water into glucose and oxygen. This occurs in the chloroplasts of plant cells, primarily using chlorophyll. The equation for photosynthesis is 6CO2 + 6H2O + light energy → C6H12O6 + 6O2. It is essential for producing food and oxygen on Earth.", "question": "What are the main inputs for photosynthesis?", "answers": [{"text": "Sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water", "label": 1}, {"text": "Nitrogen, oxygen, and heat", "label": 0}, {"text": "Minerals and soil", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The ancient Egyptian civilization flourished along the Nile River from around 3100 BCE. They built monumental structures like the pyramids at Giza, which served as tombs for pharaohs. Hieroglyphics, their writing system, was used on papyrus and stone. Agriculture was central to their economy, relying on the annual flooding of the Nile.", "question": "What was the primary purpose of the pyramids at Giza?", "answers": [{"text": "Tombs for pharaohs", "label": 1}, {"text": "Religious temples", "label": 0}, {"text": "Storage facilities", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The internet originated from ARPANET, a project funded by the US Department of Defense in the 1960s. It aimed to create a network that could withstand partial outages. Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web in 1989, introducing HTTP and HTML. Today, it connects billions of devices worldwide.", "question": "Who invented the World Wide Web?", "answers": [{"text": "Tim Berners-Lee", "label": 1}, {"text": "Bill Gates", "label": 0}, {"text": "Steve Jobs", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "In George Orwell's novel '1984', the protagonist Winston Smith lives in a dystopian society ruled by the Party and its leader Big Brother. The story explores themes of surveillance, propaganda, and totalitarianism. Winston works at the Ministry of Truth, altering historical records to fit the Party's narrative.", "question": "What is the main setting of George Orwell's '1984'?", "answers": [{"text": "A dystopian society ruled by the Party", "label": 1}, {"text": "A utopian future", "label": 0}, {"text": "Historical England", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Usain Bolt, a Jamaican sprinter, won eight Olympic gold medals in his career. He set world records in the 100m and 200m dashes. Bolt's speed and technique made him one of the most dominant athletes in track and field history. He retired in 2017 after a successful career.", "question": "How many Olympic gold medals did Usain Bolt win?", "answers": [{"text": "Eight", "label": 1}, {"text": "Five", "label": 0}, {"text": "Ten", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Mount Everest, located in the Himalayas on the border of Nepal and Tibet, is the highest mountain in the world at 8,848 meters. It was first summited in 1953 by Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay. Climbers face extreme weather and low oxygen levels when attempting to reach the peak.", "question": "In which year was Mount Everest first summited?", "answers": [{"text": "1953", "label": 1}, {"text": "1924", "label": 0}, {"text": "1965", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Malaria is a mosquito-borne disease caused by Plasmodium parasites. Symptoms include fever, chills, and flu-like illness. It is prevalent in tropical regions and can be fatal if not treated. Prevention methods include using insecticide-treated nets and taking antimalarial drugs.", "question": "What causes malaria?", "answers": [{"text": "Plasmodium parasites", "label": 1}, {"text": "Bacterial infection", "label": 0}, {"text": "Viral infection", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Vincent van Gogh, a Dutch post-impressionist painter, created over 2,000 artworks in his lifetime. His famous works include 'Starry Night' and 'Sunflowers'. Van Gogh's style featured bold colors and expressive brushwork, influenced by his emotional struggles.", "question": "What is one of Vincent van Gogh's famous paintings?", "answers": [{"text": "Starry Night", "label": 1}, {"text": "Mona Lisa", "label": 0}, {"text": "The Scream", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Mars, the fourth planet from the Sun, is known as the Red Planet due to its iron oxide surface. It has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos. NASA's rovers have explored its surface, searching for signs of past life. The planet's atmosphere is mostly carbon dioxide.", "question": "What gives Mars its red color?", "answers": [{"text": "Iron oxide", "label": 1}, {"text": "Volcanic activity", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Supply and demand is a fundamental concept in economics that determines the price of goods. When demand exceeds supply, prices rise; conversely, when supply exceeds demand, prices fall. This principle helps markets reach equilibrium.", "question": "What happens to prices when demand exceeds supply?", "answers": [{"text": "Prices rise", "label": 1}, {"text": "Prices fall", "label": 0}, {"text": "Prices remain the same", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The 2020 US presidential election was held on November 3, featuring Joe Biden and Donald Trump as main candidates. Biden won with 306 electoral votes. Voter turnout was one of the highest in recent history due to the COVID-19 pandemic.", "question": "Who won the 2020 US presidential election?", "answers": [{"text": "Joe Biden", "label": 1}, {"text": "Donald Trump", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Active learning methods, such as group discussions and hands-on activities, improve student engagement and retention. Unlike traditional lectures, these techniques encourage critical thinking and problem-solving skills.", "question": "What does active learning improve in students?", "answers": [{"text": "Engagement and retention", "label": 1}, {"text": "Memorization only", "label": 0}, {"text": "Physical fitness", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Carrots are rich in beta-carotene, which the body converts to vitamin A. This nutrient supports eye health and immune function. Eating a balanced diet with vegetables like carrots can prevent deficiencies.", "question": "What nutrient is abundant in carrots?", "answers": [{"text": "Beta-carotene", "label": 1}, {"text": "Vitamin C", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Japan, an island nation in East Asia, is known for its cherry blossoms and Mount Fuji. Its culture includes traditions like tea ceremonies and festivals. Tokyo, the capital, is a bustling metropolis with advanced technology.", "question": "What is a famous natural feature in Japan?", "answers": [{"text": "Mount Fuji", "label": 1}, {"text": "The Grand Canyon", "label": 0}, {"text": "Niagara Falls", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Ludwig van Beethoven, a German composer, bridged the Classical and Romantic eras. He composed nine symphonies, with the Fifth Symphony being one of the most famous. Beethoven began losing his hearing in his late 20s but continued to create masterpieces.", "question": "How many symphonies did Beethoven compose?", "answers": [{"text": "Nine", "label": 1}, {"text": "Five", "label": 0}, {"text": "Ten", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Cognitive dissonance occurs when a person holds conflicting beliefs or attitudes. This psychological phenomenon can lead to discomfort, prompting individuals to change their views or behaviors to resolve the inconsistency.", "question": "What is cognitive dissonance?", "answers": [{"text": "Conflicting beliefs leading to discomfort", "label": 1}, {"text": "A state of complete agreement", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Newton's first law of motion states that an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion stays in motion unless acted upon by an external force. This is also known as the law of inertia.", "question": "What is Newton's first law of motion?", "answers": [{"text": "An object at rest stays at rest unless acted upon", "label": 1}, {"text": "Every action has an equal and opposite reaction", "label": 0}, {"text": "Force equals mass times acceleration", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Pi (π) is a mathematical constant representing the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter, approximately 3.14159. It is irrational and used in formulas for circles, spheres, and trigonometry.", "question": "What does pi represent in mathematics?", "answers": [{"text": "The ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter", "label": 1}, {"text": "The square root of 2", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Diwali, the festival of lights, is celebrated by Hindus, Sikhs, and Jains. It symbolizes the victory of light over darkness and good over evil. Families light lamps, share sweets, and exchange gifts during this five-day festival.", "question": "What does Diwali symbolize?", "answers": [{"text": "Victory of light over darkness", "label": 1}, {"text": "The harvest season", "label": 0}, {"text": "New Year's Day", "label": 0}]}

{"passage": "The Great Barrier Reef, located off the coast of Australia, is the world's largest coral reef system. Composed of billions of tiny organisms called coral polyps, it stretches over 2,300 kilometers and supports a diverse array of marine life, including fish, turtles, and sharks. Climate change, particularly rising ocean temperatures, has led to widespread bleaching events where corals lose their color and vitality. Conservation efforts include marine protected areas and international agreements to reduce pollution.", "question": "What is the primary cause of coral bleaching in the Great Barrier Reef?", "answers": [{"text": "Rising ocean temperatures", "label": 1}, {"text": "Overfishing", "label": 0}, {"text": "Earthquakes", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Albert Einstein developed the theory of relativity in the early 20th century, revolutionizing our understanding of space, time, and gravity. His famous equation, E=mc², shows that energy and mass are interchangeable. Einstein was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921 for his work on the photoelectric effect, not relativity. He fled Nazi Germany in 1933 and spent his later years in the United States.", "question": "For which achievement did Albert Einstein win the Nobel Prize?", "answers": [{"text": "Work on the photoelectric effect", "label": 1}, {"text": "Theory of relativity", "label": 0}, {"text": "Development of quantum mechanics", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The Amazon Rainforest covers about 5.5 million square kilometers in South America and is home to more than 1,000 species of birds and mammals. It plays a crucial role in regulating the Earth's climate by absorbing large amounts of carbon dioxide. Deforestation for agriculture and logging has reduced its size, leading to biodiversity loss. Indigenous communities have lived there for thousands of years, relying on its resources.", "question": "What is one major role of the Amazon Rainforest in global climate regulation?", "answers": [{"text": "Absorbing carbon dioxide", "label": 1}, {"text": "Producing oxygen", "label": 0}, {"text": "Generating rainfall", "label": 0}, {"text": "Reducing sea levels", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The Wright brothers, Orville and Wilbur, achieved the first powered flight in 1903 at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina. Their airplane, the Wright Flyer, flew for 12 seconds and covered 120 feet. This invention paved the way for modern aviation. Prior to this, they experimented with gliders and bicycles to understand aerodynamics.", "question": "Where did the Wright brothers first achieve powered flight?", "answers": [{"text": "Kitty Hawk, North Carolina", "label": 1}, {"text": "Paris, France", "label": 0}, {"text": "London, England", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants convert sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water into glucose and oxygen. This occurs in the chloroplasts of plant cells, using chlorophyll to capture light energy. It is essential for producing food and maintaining oxygen levels in the atmosphere. Without photosynthesis, life on Earth as we know it would not be possible.", "question": "What are the main inputs for photosynthesis?", "answers": [{"text": "Sunlight, carbon dioxide, and water", "label": 1}, {"text": "Oxygen and nitrogen", "label": 0}, {"text": "Minerals and soil", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The Renaissance period, from the 14th to the 17th century, marked a cultural rebirth in Europe, emphasizing art, science, and humanism. Leonardo da Vinci, a key figure, created masterpieces like the Mona Lisa and advanced studies in anatomy and engineering. This era saw the invention of the printing press by Johannes Gutenberg, which spread knowledge widely.", "question": "Who invented the printing press during the Renaissance?", "answers": [{"text": "Johannes Gutenberg", "label": 1}, {"text": "Leonardo da Vinci", "label": 0}, {"text": "Michelangelo", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Volcanoes are openings in the Earth's crust that release molten rock, ash, and gases. The eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD buried the city of Pompeii under layers of ash. This event preserved the city, providing insights into ancient Roman life. Volcanic activity can be explosive or effusive, depending on the lava's viscosity.", "question": "What ancient city was buried by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius?", "answers": [{"text": "Pompeii", "label": 1}, {"text": "Rome", "label": 0}, {"text": "Athens", "label": 0}, {"text": "Sparta", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The human heart is a muscular organ that pumps blood throughout the body via the circulatory system. It consists of four chambers: two atria and two ventricles. The right side pumps deoxygenated blood to the lungs, while the left side pumps oxygenated blood to the rest of the body. Regular exercise can strengthen the heart and improve its efficiency.", "question": "How many chambers does the human heart have?", "answers": [{"text": "Four", "label": 1}, {"text": "Two", "label": 0}, {"text": "Three", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist who conducted pioneering research on radioactivity. She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize and the only person to win in two different sciences: Physics in 1903 and Chemistry in 1911. Her work led to the discovery of polonium and radium. Curie faced significant gender discrimination in her career.", "question": "In which years did Marie Curie win Nobel Prizes?", "answers": [{"text": "1903 and 1911", "label": 1}, {"text": "1895 and 1905", "label": 0}, {"text": "1910 and 1920", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The Internet evolved from ARPANET, a project funded by the U.S. Department of Defense in the 1960s. It allows global communication and information sharing through interconnected networks. Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web in 1989, making the Internet more accessible. Today, it connects billions of devices worldwide.", "question": "Who invented the World Wide Web?", "answers": [{"text": "Tim Berners-Lee", "label": 1}, {"text": "Bill Gates", "label": 0}, {"text": "Steve Jobs", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Elephants are the largest land animals, with African elephants being larger than their Asian counterparts. They live in herds led by a matriarch and communicate using infrasonic sounds. Their trunks are versatile tools for eating, drinking, and social interactions. Habitat loss and poaching threaten their populations.", "question": "What is the social structure of elephant herds?", "answers": [{"text": "Led by a matriarch", "label": 1}, {"text": "Led by the strongest male", "label": 0}, {"text": "Individual solitary living", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The Industrial Revolution began in the late 18th century in Britain, transforming economies with mechanized production. Key inventions included the steam engine by James Watt and the spinning jenny by James Hargreaves. This period saw a shift from agrarian societies to urban factories, leading to significant social changes.", "question": "Who invented the steam engine?", "answers": [{"text": "James Watt", "label": 1}, {"text": "James Hargreaves", "label": 0}, {"text": "Eli Whitney", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Renewable energy sources like solar and wind are sustainable alternatives to fossil fuels. Solar panels convert sunlight into electricity, while wind turbines harness wind energy. These sources reduce greenhouse gas emissions and combat climate change. Many countries are investing in renewables to meet energy demands.", "question": "What do solar panels convert into electricity?", "answers": [{"text": "Sunlight", "label": 1}, {"text": "Wind", "label": 0}, {"text": "Water", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The ancient Egyptian pyramids, such as the Great Pyramid of Giza, were built as tombs for pharaohs around 2580–2560 BC. They required massive labor forces and advanced engineering. The pyramids aligned with the stars, reflecting the Egyptians' astronomical knowledge. Khufu was the pharaoh for whom the Great Pyramid was constructed.", "question": "For which pharaoh was the Great Pyramid of Giza built?", "answers": [{"text": "Khufu", "label": 1}, {"text": "Tutankhamun", "label": 0}, {"text": "Ramses II", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Quantum mechanics is a branch of physics that describes the behavior of particles at atomic and subatomic levels. It introduced concepts like wave-particle duality and uncertainty principle. Niels Bohr and Erwin Schrödinger were key contributors. This theory has applications in modern technology, such as semiconductors.", "question": "Who developed the Schrödinger equation?", "answers": [{"text": "Erwin Schrödinger", "label": 1}, {"text": "Niels Bohr", "label": 0}, {"text": "Albert Einstein", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The Apollo 11 mission in 1969 landed the first humans on the Moon. Astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the lunar surface, while Michael Collins orbited above. They collected samples and planted the American flag. This achievement was a major milestone in space exploration.", "question": "Who was the first person to walk on the Moon?", "answers": [{"text": "Neil Armstrong", "label": 1}, {"text": "Buzz Aldrin", "label": 0}, {"text": "Michael Collins", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Biodiversity refers to the variety of life on Earth, including plants, animals, and microorganisms. Coral reefs, despite covering less than 1% of the ocean floor, support about 25% of all marine species. Loss of biodiversity can disrupt ecosystems and affect human life through reduced food and medicine sources.", "question": "What percentage of marine species do coral reefs support?", "answers": [{"text": "About 25%", "label": 1}, {"text": "About 50%", "label": 0}, {"text": "About 10%", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Charles Darwin's theory of evolution by natural selection, published in 1859, explains how species adapt over time. He developed this idea after observing finches in the Galápagos Islands. Natural selection favors traits that improve survival and reproduction. This theory forms the basis of modern biology.", "question": "Where did Charles Darwin observe finches that influenced his theory?", "answers": [{"text": "Galápagos Islands", "label": 1}, {"text": "Hawaii", "label": 0}, {"text": "Australia", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "Vaccines work by stimulating the immune system to recognize and fight specific diseases. The first vaccine, for smallpox, was developed by Edward Jenner in 1796. Modern vaccines have eradicated diseases like polio in many parts of the world. They are a key tool in public health strategies.", "question": "Who developed the first vaccine?", "answers": [{"text": "Edward Jenner", "label": 1}, {"text": "Louis Pasteur", "label": 0}, {"text": "Alexander Fleming", "label": 0}]} 
{"passage": "The human brain consists of regions like the cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem, each with specific functions. The cerebrum handles thinking and voluntary movements, while the brainstem controls basic life functions like breathing. Neuroplasticity allows the brain to adapt and form new connections throughout life.", "question": "Which part of the brain handles thinking and voluntary movements?", "answers": [{"text": "Cerebrum", "label": 1}, {"text": "Cerebellum", "label": 0}, {"text": "Brainstem", "label": 0}]}

